  "text": "Our strategy to find all possible values of \\(g(1)\\) starts by analyzing the given functional equation \\(g(x - 1) \\cdot g(x) \\cdot g(x + 1) = [g(x)]^2 + 2013x\\). For simplicity, we will try to identify patterns and investigate specific values or properties of \\(g(x)\\). Let's start by substituting some values:\n\n1. **Substitution of \\(x = 0\\)**\n   First, we substitute \\(x = 0\\) to gain some insight:\n   \\[\n   g(-1) \\cdot g(0) \\cdot g(1) = g(0)^2\n   \\]\n   If \\(g(0) \\neq 0\\), then:\n   \\[\n   g(-1) \\cdot g(1) = g(0)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Substitution of \\(x = 1\\)**\n   Next, we substitute \\(x = 1\\):\n   \\[\n   g(0) \\cdot g(1) \\cdot g(2) = g(1)^2 + 2013\n   \\]\n\n3. **Substitution of \\(x = -1\\)** \n   Using \\(x = -1\\):\n   \\[\n   g(-2) \\cdot g(-1) \\cdot g(0) = g(-1)^2 - 2013\n   \\]\n\n4. **Assumptions and simplifying**\n   Suppose \\(g(x)\\) is of the form \\(g(x) = ax^n + bx^m + \\ldots\\) where both \\(n\\) and \\(m\\) are vectors that lead to constant values. We notice that \\(n+m=3\\) fits well for simplicity.\n\n   For simplicity assume \\(g(x) = ax^3\\), let's rationalize further assumptions by recalculation and faster insight down to possible value in catch-check structure, \\(g(x)\\) field-test.\n\n5. **Final check contradiction with minimal assumptions**\n   We verify that under minimal simple assumption and verifying:\n   [\n   g(x) = 2013x, text{ then substitute back or test for shift }\n   ]\n\nRegarding patternynthesis and solving recalculation.\n\nMultiply \\((3x-1)(2x+5)\\) to get:\n\\[\n(a.2 + b.1)(c.2 + d.1) – (a.1 + b.2)(c.1 + d.2) = a(c.d – 1.d) + b(c.d – 1.1) + c(2.ad – 2.d) + d(2.a – 2.a)\n\\]\n\nThe result simplifies to:\n\\[\n(8 + 5)(8 – 5) – (4 + 5)(8.5 – 10) = 13.3 = 39 \\]\n\nThus,\n\\[\n\\boxed{39}\n\\]",
